{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R4TdTqUQu6xRj23prCMuut","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Decisions\n2\t\n3\t## D001 — 2026-09-19: clean project and engine-first compatibility probe\n4\t\n5\tObserved: `/Users/elinhaggstrom/Nortropic Runtime` was empty and not a Git repo.\n6\tThe requested private remote is `Nortropic/nortropic-runtime`; authenticated lookup\n7\tfound no accessible repository with that name. Create new history, never overwrite.\n8\tKeep original mandate once in this directory. No older project imported.\n9\t\n10\tFirst engine candidate: upstream `openai/symphony`, observed HEAD\n11\t`be10a1b79df723d6d7612b5651c8522704dafb2e`. Inspect pinned code, dependencies,\n12\ttracker and executor interface before installing. Do not reimplement SPEC.md.\n13\t\n14\t## D002 — 2026-09-19: subscription-only executor checks\n15\t\n16\tObserved outside sandbox: Codex CLI 0.147.0 reports ChatGPT authentication;\n17\tClaude Code 2.1.257 reports `claude.ai`, subscription `max`. No API-key/provider\n18\tenvironment override was present. Sandbox-only auth checks misleadingly failed\n19\tbecause network/keychain access was unavailable. No model run yet.\n20\t\n21\tClaude is older than mandate's direct AGENTS support version. Use only `@AGENTS.md`\n22\tin CLAUDE.md and test actual loading at root and subdirectory. Global user settings\n23\tcontain historic project permissions; these do not grant Runtime additional rights.\n24\tNo global settings or hooks changed. Relevant managed settings still need checking.\n25\t\n26\tSources consulted: [Codex authentication](https://developers.openai.com/codex/auth/),\n27\t[app-server](https://developers.openai.com/codex/app-server/),\n28\t[AGENTS hierarchy](https://developers.openai.com/codex/guides/agents-md/),\n29\t[Claude memory](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory),\n30\t[Symphony](https://github.com/openai/symphony).\n31\t\n32\t## D003 — 2026-09-19: distinguish backup from integration\n33\t\n34\tInitial project records may be preserved before runtime acceptance. Subsequent\n35\tcandidate deliveries need relevant verification and separate review. GitHub org\n36\tAPI reports Free plan. Server integration guarantees remain untested; do not\n37\tclaim branch protection is enforceable until tested. A local review is not a\n38\tserver permission boundary.\n39\t\n40\t\n41\t## D004 — 2026-09-19: private origin and local dependency updates\n42\t\n43\tPrivate remote and first push verified; see evidence/startup. Server returns 403\n44\tfor branch protection on the current Free organization. Owner asked about making\n45\tit public; recommended retaining privacy and GitHub Team (listed $4/user/month,\n46\tactual seats/tax must be confirmed in billing). Decision pending; no paid plan or\n47\tvisibility change made. Sources: https://github.com/team and\n48\thttps://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-protected-branches.\n49\t\n50\tFirst Codex root probe failed: installed 0.147.0 cannot use requested gpt-6-astra.\n51\tDo not substitute model or repeat unchanged. Official release 0.155.1 will be\n52\tinstalled project-locally and checksummed before one new attempt. Global CLI and\n53\tother projects remain untouched. This is a technical prerequisite adjustment,\n54\tnot new API billing.\n55\t\n56\t\n57\t## D005 — 2026-09-19: Claude subscription access is blocked server-side\n58\t\n59\tAuth status was insufficient evidence. First real read-only CLI run returned\n60\tHTTP 403 `oauth_org_not_allowed`, saying the organization disabled subscription\n61\taccess for Claude Code. Raw evidence: evidence/startup/claude-root. Reported usage:\n62\t0 input/output tokens, total_cost_usd 0. Treat the result's is_error=true and\n63\tterminal_reason=api_error as failure despite subtype=success. No repeat or API-key\n64\tfallback. Owner asked to check existing subscription access; remain WAITING_ACCESS\n65\tuntil changed evidence. Codex/engine work can continue independently.\n66\t\n67\t\n68\t## D006 — 2026-09-19: owner authorizes public repository\n69\t\n70\tOwner explicitly said the repository need not be private and made it PUBLIC;\n71\tverified live with gh repo view. This supersedes the original privacy constraint\n72\tfor this repository, not permissions to publish other projects or secrets. No\n73\tpaid GitHub upgrade is required. Original mandate is retained unchanged as source.\n74\t\n75\tProposed main protection: PR required, no force push/deletion, administrators\n76\tincluded, up-to-date candidate, runtime/tests and runtime/review required. Zero\n77\tGitHub account approvals because separate agent review is issued by the trusted\n78\thost as an exact-SHA status; it is not a second GitHub identity. Separate agent\n79\tprocess is not itself a security boundary. Until restricted candidate access and\n80\ttrusted status production are tested, this is only the server gate, not §5E.\n81\tConfiguration: config/branch-protection.json.\n82\t\n83\t\n84\t## D007 — 2026-09-19: failed protection activation and corrected process limiter\n85\t\n86\tFirst GitHub protection PUT returned HTTP422 because the payload supplied both\n87\tcontexts and checks. A wrongly chained negative push continued and advanced main\n88\tfrom f2863df to 4dec677 before protection existed. This was an operator error and\n89\tis NOT approved integration evidence. No force push/rewrite was performed. Raw\n90\toutputs remain in evidence/integration/missing-checks-push.*. Corrected payload\n91\tuses only checks. PUT and verified readback succeeded; separate harmless same-tree\n92\tcommit 3e894eef was rejected by GitHub (PR required; 2 checks expected), main\n93\tremained 4dec677. Future dependent mutation probes require successful checked\n94\tpreconditions, executed separately or subprocess check=True.\n95\t\n96\tSeparate reviewer then found that bounded.py could leave TERM-ignoring children\n97\talive and did not handle SIGTERM to the helper. Corrected shared cleanup checks\n98\tthe process group even after leader exit, escalates KILL, handles INT/TERM, and\n99\trecords removal before success. Three real-process regression cases pass (normal\n100\tleader exit, timeout, SIGTERM). Limit is configured run time plus up to 4 seconds\n101\tcleanup; children deliberately escaping the group and SIGKILL of the limiter are\n102\tnot claimed covered. Review and remaining results must be tied to next candidate.\n103\t\n104\t## D008 — 2026-09-19: narrow engine probe, no general replacement engine\n105\t\n106\tUse unmodified official Symphony binary and GitHub adapter; wrapper only confines\n107\tits Codex protocol boundary and records the experiment. Remove github_api dynamic\n108\ttool because the available host token has broad rights. Disable desktop/app MCPs\n109\tin this invocation, retain global safety rules and subscription auth. Candidate\n110\twrites only isolated workspace, no network, no additional /tmp write roots.\n111\tHost hook independently checks known JSON and preserves before tracker transition.\n112\tOne durable launch marker prevents blind model retries; a bounded experiment\n113\tlauncher holds one lock. This is deliberately NOT durable production orchestration\n114\tor a claim that §5D/E are satisfied. Acceptance and baseline: docs/probe-b1.md.\n115\t\n116\tSource inspection also identifies unresolved engine gaps: retry/blocked maps\n117\tinitialize empty (orchestrator.ex:24–75); cleanup ignores before_remove failure\n118\t(workspace.ex:159–162,331–352); AgentRunner directly depends on Codex.AppServer.\n119\tAfter the small live probe, assess whether narrow integration can cover the mandate\n120\tor choose a smaller reuse route. Do not incrementally build a general replacement.\n121\t\n122\t\n123\t## D009 — 2026-09-19: B1 attempt 1 failed before model; bounded compatibility fix\n124\t\n125\tSymphony found GH-1, created workspace, started app-server, called after_run and\n126\tpreserved missing-result FAIL, then host removed dispatch label. No model turn\n127\tstarted: Codex 0.155.1 rejected upstream's legacy approvalPolicy reject (-32600);\n128\tits generated schema accepts never or granular. Raw traces are under\n129\tevidence/motor-probe-attempt-1. Engine returned 0 while verifier failed; no success\n130\tclaimed. Process group removal measured true, no residual matching workers found.\n131\t\n132\tAttempt 2 explicitly changes policy encoding to never (no approval requests,\n133\tsandbox retained), supported by generated 0.155.1 schema. Preserve attempt 1 and\n134\trecord attempt=2 in the contract and launch metadata. No automatic retry; no\n135\tmodel usage in failed attempt. Same task, acceptance and artifacts remain.\n136\t\n137\t\n138\t## D010 — 2026-09-19: review discovers built-in MCP exposure in attempt 2\n139\t\n140\tSeparate result reviewer found cua_repl and codex_apps startup ready notifications\n141\tin the actual protocol, despite named MCP/plugin configuration disable flags.\n142\tArtifact and coordination proof remain valid; restricted-profile acceptance fails.\n143\tNo completed MCP call appears. Profile is disabled in contract and launch paths.\n144\tCorrect built-in feature flags (apps/computer_use/browser variants) per installed\n145\tCLI feature list, then query mcpServerStatus/list in a fresh thread without a\n146\tmodel turn. Do not rerun unchanged or describe intended isolation as observed.\n147\tAlso corrected quota readout from initial 2% to final 3%, without attribution.\n148\t\n149\tThe first no-model correction removed codex_apps but still loaded plugin cua_repl\n150\t(evidence/startup/worker-profile-inventory.json). Disabling plugin loading for this\n151\tworker invocation, in addition to built-in app/browser/computer features, yielded\n152\tonly three disabled named servers with zero tools; fresh-thread full inventory\n153\tpassed (worker-profile-inventory-fixed.json), with no model turn. Global settings,\n154\tmanaged rules and hook configuration remain unchanged; this profile contains no\n155\trequired safety plugin. The old fixture remains disabled; any later live profile\n156\tclaim must refer to its actual invocation, not retroactively rewrite attempt 2.\n157\t\n158\t\n159\t## D011 — 2026-09-19: evaluate existing durable engine before more Symphony layers\n160\t\n161\tThe source gaps in D008 are core requirements, not failures of the fixture. A\n162\tSymphony-specific persistence/state/Claude orchestration layer risks becoming a\n163\tsecond general engine. Evaluate Temporal as a replacement candidate in one bounded\n164\tlocal no-model probe; do not run both orchestrators as the product or adopt a new\n165\tproduct scope. Same development task/permissions/subscription mandate remains.\n166\t\n167\tSelected experimental dependencies: official Temporal CLI1.9.1 arm64 and Python\n168\tSDK temporalio1.33.0 (Python>=3.10). No extras, cloud account, subscription or API\n169\tusage. Local server supports an explicit SQLite persistence file; default without\n170\tthat flag is volatile. start-dev is documented for development, not production,\n171\tand omits some HTTP security checks. Bind loopback and disable UI, no candidate\n172\tnetwork access if later selected. Durability claim still requires actual restart\n173\tevidence; activity side effects and process fencing remain integration concerns.\n174\t\n175\tSources consulted: https://docs.temporal.io/cli/command-reference/server (flags and\n176\tdevelopment limits), https://docs.temporal.io/develop/python/best-practices/error-handling\n177\t(timeouts/retry policy), https://docs.temporal.io/develop/python/workflows/message-passing\n178\t(durable signals). Official release metadata and PyPI package metadata checked.\n179\tDecision is only to run a compatibility experiment, not yet to adopt the engine.\n180\t\n181\t\n182\t## D012 — 2026-09-19: select Temporal for next bounded execution slice\n183\t\n184\tComparison against required properties: Symphony has measured GitHub discovery and\n185\tCodex dispatch but volatile retry/waiting maps, no provider boundary, and cleanup\n186\tthat proceeds despite failed preservation hook. Filling all three with host-side\n187\tstate would duplicate orchestration. Temporal's existing history/signals/replay\n188\tpreserved completed work and waiting in the actual abrupt-restart probe (9b770d1);\n189\tPython activities are a documented arbitrary executor boundary. This removes the\n190\tneed to implement scheduler persistence or a general Codex↔Claude protocol server.\n191\t\n192\tChoose Temporal for the next small Codex-connected task. It replaces Symphony as\n193\tthe engine candidate, not a second running orchestrator. Reuse B1 learnings and\n194\tverified worker configuration; keep Symphony evidence and disabled experiment.\n195\tTask input remains accepted outcome/underlay/permissions/acceptance; native engine\n196\tworkflow owns state. No second method schema/status registry or business logic.\n197\t\n198\tThis is limited technical replanning under mandate§2: same scope, no new billing,\n199\tno new external rights. Local pinned dependencies only. Existing dev-server SQLite\n200\tprofile limits v0.1 to a single trusted local host if qualified; production is not\n201\ta promised goal. Candidate workspace isolation, attempt semantics, side-effect\n202\treconciliation, independent review and exact GitHub integration remain to be built\n203\tand tested in narrow activities. Do not claim Temporal automatically supplies them.\n204\tIf connecting those requires another generic engine, revisit instead of expanding.\n205\t\n206\t\n207\t## D013 — 2026-09-19: first useful accepted task and candidate boundary\n208\t\n209\tCustomer Zero task is a deterministic JSONL run-report CLI for actual provider\n210\tevidence. Current manual extraction and Claude success-subtype/error conflict\n211\tcreate a concrete false-pass risk. Use three meaningful implementation phases\n212\t(Codex parsing, Claude parsing, Codex final CLI verification), one workspace and\n213\tTemporal-owned task history. Claude phase waits on known server denial D005; no\n214\tAPI fallback or repeated denied call. The first delivery connects only Codex.\n215\t\n216\tUse Codex's native named permissions profile with minimal reads, isolated workspace\n217\twrites and network disabled, plus disabled apps/MCP/plugins. Host acceptance and\n218\tGitHub authority remain outside candidate workspace. Test actual denial with\n219\tnonsecret canaries before a live turn. Official reference consulted:\n220\thttps://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/config-file/config-reference (permissions filesystem\n221\tand network options); local 0.155.1 CLI/schema decides invocation compatibility.\n222\tNo assertion of complete rights separation until observed, including escape routes.\n223\t\n224\tThe first thread preflight exposed a real compatibility error: restricted reads\n225\tblocked ancestor AGENTS discovery. Added read-only grants for AGENTS.md and\n226\tAGENTS.override.md at ancestor paths; no directory-wide host read grant. Fresh\n227\tthread now reports activePermissionProfile nr, writable roots exactly tools and\n228\t.scratch, no network/tmp writes, and only disabled MCP servers with zero tools.\n229\tThe six harmless boundary checks also passed with this corrected profile. No model\n230\tturn was used for either inventory. Raw evidence: evidence/accepted-task/profile-*\n231\tand boundary-instructions.json. CLI sandbox syntax is `codex sandbox`, not the old\n232\t`codex sandbox macos`; the mistaken help invocation failed before doing any work.\n233\t\n234\t## D014 — 2026-09-19: stop on verifier escape; preserve attempt and repair boundary\n235\t\n236\tSeparate review of f61b84d found a real host-write escape: candidate-owned\n237\t.scratch/sample.jsonl could be a symlink followed by host write_text. It also found\n238\tcheck/use races in candidate hashing/copying. Interrupted live attempt1 by SIGTERM\n239\tbefore acceptance, preserving raw events and source. Attempt result: interrupted,\n240\t34.166s, no terminal usage (unknown, not0), provider group removed. Workflow reached\n241\twaiting_diagnosis, attempt1 and same result survived server/worker restart. No\n242\tacceptance occurred and no provider completion claimed; all four engine groups\n243\tremoved. evidence/accepted-task/engine and attempt-1 retain raw negative results.\n244\t\n245\tCorrection: pass CLI data through stdin, never host-write in candidate-owned paths.\n246\tOpen every candidate path component with O_NOFOLLOW, require bounded regular fd,\n247\tread/hash those bytes and freeze them in a new host-owned evidence directory.\n248\tExecute acceptance against that snapshot with source read-only and no network.\n249\tKill/reap verifier process groups on completion/timeout. Regression tests show CLI\n250\tstdin leaves candidate symlink target unchanged, frozen source write denied, and\n251\tsymlink/file-parent/FIFO snapshot refusal. These are bounded host-file-handling\n252\tproofs, not a claim to fence deliberately detached hostile process trees.\n253\t\n254\tResume the SAME workflow/candidate only after correction review, with an explicit\n255\tnative diagnosis signal carrying changed prerequisite. Attempt number increments\n256\trather than restarting history. Driver checks old recorded PIDs/group absence.\n257\tNo automatic repeated model attempt and no Claude call while D005 unchanged.\n258\t\n259\t## D015 — 2026-09-19: owner defers Claude usage purchase\n260\t\n261\tOwner plans to buy more Claude Code usage at a later time. Recommendation accepted\n262\tfor work planning: no need to buy now to continue independent Codex, recovery,\n263\treview and integration work. No purchase, API fallback or periodic Claude calls.\n264\tReal executor swap and final v0.1 acceptance remain pending, not waived. Observed\n265\tserver error D005 was oauth_org_not_allowed (subscription access disabled), not a\n266\tquota-exhaustion result; check correct account/organization access before assuming\n267\tadditional usage will resolve it. Ask owner only when an actual access/cost choice\n268\tis needed, not for routine continuation.\n269\t\n270\t## D016 — 2026-09-19: reuse publication checks, not old orchestration\n271\t\n272\tConcrete need: reject stale/missing/failed review or test evidence at the actual\n273\tcandidate publication boundary. Read only selected files in\n274\t/Users/elinhaggstrom/kernel-arbete at clean revision\n275\t2dfc58f362db100c179b7b738e9a720dae39a256:\n276\t- tests/scripts/nortropic-codex-autopilot/publication-callers.py\n277\t- scripts/nortropic-codex-autopilot.py publish/publication_authority sections.\n278\t\n279\tUseful test ideas: every real publication caller supplies mandatory authority;\n280\tmissing/wrong authority rejects; re-read exact base/head after push; final merged\n281\ttree must match tested candidate. Reuse these cases in Runtime-specific tests.\n282\tNo old PASS imported; old tests were not executed. The old implementation depends\n283\ton its roadmap stages, canonical tasks.spec and normal two-parent merge, whereas\n284\tRuntime keeps acceptance outside candidate write access and uses protected linear\n285\tintegration. Copying that engine would add irrelevant coupling. No source imported.\n286\tThe briefly inspected nortropic-review.js is site/design/SEO-specific and has\n287\tmultiple verifier fan-outs; it is not appropriate for this neutral small code task.\n288\tOriginals remained untouched; no prior permissions/workflow mandates inherited.\n289\t\n290\tNext slice: bind whole-task completion, tests and independent review to exact\n291\tcandidate objects before any publishing action. A passed first phase must not\n292\tpublish the unfinished report task. Keep its native WAITING_ACCESS state intact.\n293\tThen connect the same boundary to a second, Codex-only accepted task.\n294\t\n295\t## D017 — 2026-09-19: connect a second useful task without waiting for Claude\n296\t\n297\tPR5 integrated the separately reviewed publication boundary. Next connect it to\n298\tnative workflow activities: accepted task input, bounded implementation, frozen\n299\tGit candidate, host acceptance, fresh independent read-only Codex review, then\n300\tprotected publication. Existing report workflow remains waiting for Claude; it\n301\tmust replay unchanged and must not publish as a completed task.\n302\t\n303\tSecond useful task will verify evidence file hashes with a small standard-library\n304\tCLI, replacing repeated manual backup/candidate hash checking. Its accepted scope\n305\tand verifier will be frozen before invocation. This remains Runtime development\n306\tCustomer Zero, not a new method platform. Both tasks use the same DevelopmentTask\n307\tworkflow and local Temporal engine. No new API billing/dependencies/permissions.\n308\t\n309\tGeneralize only task parameters and the missing reviewed activities. Preserve the\n310\told workflow history using Temporal's native Replayer before running revised code.\n311\tA new central SQLite location may be established by backup after all writers stop;\n312\tretain original database, verify existing waiting workflow, and run only one local\n313\tserver. Authority/test/review artifacts remain host-owned outside candidate write\n314\taccess. A live automatic merge is not claimed until this wiring is actually run.\n315\t\n316\t## D018 — 2026-09-19: prove bounded abrupt-worker recovery with existing primitives\n317\t\n318\tActual one-step task completed and automatically integrated via PR7; PR8 preserved\n319\tindependently reviewed evidence/runbook. Next tested abrupt SIGKILL of a native\n320\tworker with a harmless sandbox provider under the real attempt guardian. Existing\n321\tflock, recorded-process inspection, guardian deadline, native history and retry1\n322\tpolicy prevented concurrent/duplicate execution; explicit diagnosis resumed the\n323\tsame preserved work as attempt2. No new production recovery layer was needed.\n324\t\n325\tAn inherited-FD prototype worked mechanically but was rejected because candidate\n326\ttool descriptor exposure is not qualified. Keep authority outside candidate tools.\n327\tDo not claim arbitrary guardian SIGKILL/detached hostile descendants are covered.\n328\t\n329\tOne concrete service bug was exposed: preflight plain bind treated stopped-service\n330\tTIME_WAIT as active. Use SO_REUSEADDR like the actual listener, no SO_REUSEPORT;\n331\treal socket tests still refuse active listeners. Corrected native capacity-wait\n332\trestart passed with zero provider calls. Capacity was supplied as a known fixture\n333\treason, not inferred from an actual vendor quota. Raw boundaries/limits and native\n334\tbackups: evidence/recovery-probe/result.md. Claude D015 remains unchanged.\n335\t\n336\t## D019 — 2026-09-19: qualified native Claude file-tool profile\n337\t\n338\tOwner restored Claude and explicitly closed the interactive project writer.\n339\tExisting claude.ai/firstParty Max works with Claude Code2.1.257 and configured\n340\tmodel family claude-fable-5-1. No API key, new billing or changed global/managed\n341\tpolicy. Pin the observed binary SHA256 in runtime/claude_profile.py; a changed\n342\tbinary requires qualification. No package update was needed.\n343\t\n344\tManaged Bash sandbox is disabled, so use native --restricted with Read/Edit/Write\n345\tonly, exact accepted-file Edit grants, dontAsk, empty strict MCP, no Chrome/slash\n346\tcommands/plugins. Host runs tests after provider cleanup. Actual allowed write,\n347\tcontext/unaccepted denial, external read/new-write denial and symlink read denial\n348\tpassed. Existing external/symlink writes stop at the read-first precondition;\n349\tthe necessary reads are denied. These proofs cover this file-tool profile,\n350\tnot arbitrary shell execution or a hostile host. Raw events and canary hashes:\n351\tevidence/claude-qualification/verification.json.\n352\t\n353\tAutomatic @AGENTS.md import passed from root but failed from tools/ because the\n354\tparent import is external to that working directory and existing consent is\n355\tabsent. Restricted mode did not automatically inject project instructions.\n356\tUse the documented native --append-system-prompt-file pointing at the SAME\n357\tAGENTS.md source, preserving the default system prompt. Real tools-disabled probes\n358\trecovered its unique marker and plan path from both directories. No instruction\n359\tcopy/sync system, trust relaxation, telemetry activation or hook policy change.\n360\tThe fixture's instruction file is an isolated snapshot for a one-off canary.\n361\t\n362\tSources checked against actual CLI probes: official CLI reference\n363\thttps://code.claude.com/docs/en/cli-reference (restricted/file prompt loading),\n364\thttps://code.claude.com/docs/en/permissions (dontAsk and resolved file bounds),\n365\thttps://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory (external-import consent).\n366\tReported total_cost_usd is retained as CLI list-price usage metadata, not a claim\n367\tof money charged under the existing subscription. Initial failures remain visible.\n368\t\n369\t## D020 — 2026-09-19: native access signal preserves the original task\n370\t\n371\tPR10 qualified Claude without new billing/rights. Add execute_claude through the\n372\tsame process guardian, lock, fixed deadline, frozen candidate, acceptance, review\n373\tand Publisher. Require pinned CLI and existing Max auth before invocation. Parse\n374\tone successful matching session/result with explicit false is_error, completed\n375\tterminal reason and exact qualified tool inventory; never trust subtype alone.\n376\t\n377\tThe report's original accepted base9278081 is stale after support deliveries.\n378\tA host-owned continuation explicitly changes that base and replaces phase-only\n379\tverification with the already accepted full contract. Outcome, ID,